---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import TargetHeartRateCalculator from '@components/islands/TargetHeartRateCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('health', 'target-heart-rate-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How do I calculate my target heart rate?', answer: 'First estimate your maximum heart rate as 220 minus your age. Your training zones are then percentages of that maximum — for example, 50–70% for moderate exercise. Adding your resting heart rate lets the calculator use the more accurate Karvonen (heart-rate reserve) method.' },
  { question: 'What is a target heart rate zone?', answer: 'A target zone is the heart-rate range that keeps you at a chosen exercise intensity. Moderate activity is roughly 50–70% of your maximum heart rate and vigorous activity 70–85%. Training within a zone keeps effort where you intend it.' },
  { question: 'What is a normal maximum heart rate?', answer: 'The common estimate is 220 minus your age, so about 190 bpm at 30 and 170 bpm at 50. It is only an average — individual maximums vary by 10–20 bpm — but it is a reliable starting point for setting zones.' },
  { question: 'What is the Karvonen method?', answer: 'It calculates target heart rate using your heart-rate reserve (maximum minus resting), which accounts for your individual fitness level and gives more personalised zones than a simple percentage of maximum. The formula is: target = resting + intensity% × (max − resting).' },
  { question: 'What are heart rate zones for?', answer: 'Different intensities train different systems. Lower zones (60–70%) build endurance and use fat for fuel; higher zones (80–90%) improve cardiovascular fitness and speed. Training across zones is more effective than always working at one intensity.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  intro="Find your maximum heart rate and personalised training zones for warm-up, fat burn, aerobic, anaerobic and maximum-effort exercise."
  faqs={faqs}
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-07-18' }}
>
  <TargetHeartRateCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>Training by heart rate</h2>
    <p>Your maximum heart rate is estimated as <strong>220 minus your age</strong>. The zones below express training intensities as percentages of that maximum. Enter your resting heart rate for more accurate, personalised zones using the Karvonen method.</p>

    <h2>The five training zones</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Zone</th><th>% of max HR</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Warm-up</td><td>50–60%</td><td>Recovery, warm-up, cool-down</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fat burn</td><td>60–70%</td><td>Base endurance; fat as fuel</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Aerobic</td><td>70–80%</td><td>Cardiovascular fitness</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Anaerobic</td><td>80–90%</td><td>Speed and performance</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Maximum</td><td>90–100%</td><td>Short, all-out efforts</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>At <strong>age 30</strong>, maximum heart rate ≈ 220 − 30 = <strong>190 bpm</strong>. A moderate 50–70% zone is about <strong>95–133 bpm</strong>. Using Karvonen with a resting rate of 60 bpm, the 70% target becomes 60 + 0.70 × (190 − 60) = <strong>151 bpm</strong> — higher, because it factors in your fitness. That personalisation is why the resting-rate field is worth filling in.</p>

    <h2>Which zone should I train in?</h2>
    <p>Easy days should feel easy (fat-burn/aerobic zones), while hard intervals belong in the anaerobic zone — most balanced training mixes both. To gauge running effort by pace instead, see the <a href="/health/pace-calculator">pace calculator</a>.</p>
    <p>Consult a doctor before beginning intense exercise, especially if you have a heart condition. This is general information, not medical advice.</p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
